{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Mas, de fato, vosso sangue, etc.</b> Uma advertência contra enforcar-se a si mesmo. E disseram em Bereshit Rabbah: ״יכול אפי' כחנניה מישאל ועזריה״ — ת\"ל ״אך״. Ou seja: poderia-se pensar até mesmo como aqueles que se entregaram para santificar o Nome — que seria permitido a alguém ferir-se a si mesmo se teme não conseguir suportar a prova; ת\"ל ״אך״ — somente em tempo de perseguição religiosa pode alguém entregar-se à morte e matar-se a si mesmo. E assim com Shaul filho de Kish, que disse ao seu escudeiro: ״Draw your sword and run me through with it, etc.״ — ת\"ל ״אך״, significando que se uma pessoa teme que possam infligir-lhe torturas severas que não conseguiria suportar e resistir à prova, pode matar-se a si mesmo. E daqui trazem a prova para aqueles que degolam a criança no tempo de um decreto. E alguns proíbem, e explicam assim: ״יכול כחנניה וחבריו״ que had already been delivered to death — ת\"ל ״אך״ — but one may not kill oneself. ״יכול כשאול״ who delivered himself to death — ת\"ל ״אך״ — meaning one may not injure oneself at all; and Saul did so without the Sages' permission. This is from Maharam bar Abraham, nicknamed Ochman. And there was an incident involving a certain rabbi who slaughtered many children at the time of a religious persecution, for he feared they would make them transgress their faith. And there was another rabbi with him who was extremely angry with him and called him a murderer, but he paid no heed. And that rabbi said: if his words are correct, may that rabbi be killed an unusual death. And so it was: gentiles seized him and they flayed his skin and placed brimstone between the skin and the flesh, and afterward the decree was annulled. And had he not slaughtered those children, they would have survived."], :book "genesis", :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :pt, :chapter 9, :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:35:28.573810248Z", :translated-from :en, :model "glm-5.2"}